WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.034
Where could floodwater go?

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:08.308
I’m a student, and I built TerraScope to explore
that question along Bihar’s Gandak River.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:13.734
I scrub from September twenty-eighth to October
second.

00:00:13.974 --> 00:00:21.717
ERA5 reconstructs rainfall. GloFAS models river
flow. The landscape stays fixed as the readings
change.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:27.469
In Explore, the terrain screen flags low spots
near Sonpur.

00:00:27.709 --> 00:00:32.447
A field team can use this list to decide where to
survey for temporary water storage.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:37.774
I open one depression and check its footprint and
estimated volume up to the spill rim.

00:00:38.014 --> 00:00:42.567
The card shows that usable storage is still
unknown and suitability is unassessed.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.374
The cross-section shows where the terrain dips.

00:00:48.614 --> 00:00:54.287
Before relying on it, I’d ask a field team to
survey the ground and check how water actually
drains.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:00.034
I asked my AI agent to deploy TerraScope with
Embarko and give me a public link.

00:01:00.274 --> 00:01:06.072
The agent built and deployed the prototype, so
someone else can open the same map and inspect
the evidence.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.024
Try TerraScope at the address on screen.

00:01:12.264 --> 00:01:16.798
Replay the dates, open a candidate, and see what
you’d check in the field.
